From 8a120fabba2552227ac66d3800aa546052b22c0c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Michaud <jmichaud@igalia.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 10:19:57 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 18/23] [mya] Read a snapshot's types from its debug info
 through liblldb Need the bug URL (OOPS!).

Reviewed by NOBODY (OOPS!).

The layer the heap walk reads the target through: C++ types and values of a
snapshot, described by the target's own debug info, with no RTTI and no
cooperation from the target.

- SnapshotDebugInfo opens the debug info of a snapshot's images through
  liblldb. liblldb is only ever given files, never a process: each image is
  added at its load address with its UUID (Darwin) or build-id (Linux), so
  liblldb refuses a file that was rebuilt since the target loaded it rather
  than misreading memory with its layouts. It finds types and global
  variables by name, the symbol at an address, and an object's dynamic type:
  the destructor in the object's vtable, matched against the destructors'
  linkage names, gives its complete class.
- TargetType is a type of the debug info by its layout: size, fields with
  their offsets, bases (virtual ones too), pointees, array elements,
  enumerators; one per canonical type, owned by the SnapshotDebugInfo.
- TargetValue is an address and a TargetType. Nothing is read until a scalar
  is asked for, so reaching a field of a large object reads only that field.
  A failure reports once and makes the value invalid, and everything after
  it is quietly invalid, so a chain of lookups reports only the step that
  failed.
- Remote<T> is a TargetValue named by the C++ type T a walker knows it
  holds, with RemoteTraits<T> saying what it holds, the way visitChildren
  does for a cell: pointers, and readers for WTF's Vector, HashSet, HashMap
  and SentinelLinkedList, bounded by new CorpseLimits.

The DebugInfo suite (replacing the old Typeinfo suite) checks all of this in
and out of process, after the target has exited, and after its executable
was replaced on disk: same-image and cross-image objects, diamond
inheritance, destructors defined later, system-library objects, alignment,
null and unreadable pointers, and WTF containers, including deleted hash
table buckets.

Needs: liblldb at build time (MYA_HEAP), the dSYM and DWARF-shape build
patches for the types to be complete, and the HashTraits patch for the
deleted-bucket case.

